WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:02.920
This video was made possible by Dashlane.

00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:07.680
Stay safe online for free by signing up at
Dashlane.com/Wendover.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:09.730
This is Hong Kong.

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:15.190
177 years ago, as the conclusion of the First
Opium War, the United Kingdom and China signed

00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:19.340
a treaty that read, “His Majesty the Emperor
of China cedes to Her Majesty the Queen of

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:23.740
Great Britain, etc., the Island of Hong-Kong,
to be possessed in perpetuity by her Britannic

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:27.910
Majesty, Her Heirs and Successors, and to
be governed by such Laws and Regulations as

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:31.939
Her Majesty the Queen of Great Britain, etc.,
shall see fit to direct.”

00:00:31.939 --> 00:00:35.310
With that, the British empire got just a little
bit bigger.

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:40.460
The population of the island in that year
was reported as 7,450.

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:44.670
Nobody would have imagined what this island,
dotted only by a few fishing villages, would

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:45.670
become.

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:48.370
Fast forward 18 years: another Opium War,
another treaty.

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:51.820
“It has now been agreed between the Governments
of Great Britain and China that the limits

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:54.290
of British territory shall be enlarged under
lease.”

00:00:54.290 --> 00:00:57.170
Fast forward 38 years: the third and final
agreement.

00:00:57.170 --> 00:01:00.580
“It has now been agreed between the Governments
of Great Britain and China that the limits

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:04.360
of British territory shall be enlarged under
lease to the extent indicated generally on

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:05.420
the annexed map…

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:08.170
The term of this lease shall be ninety-nine
years.”

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:12.470
Ninety-nine years: as good as forever to the
signers of this agreement.

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:16.600
This forever, though, had an end and that
end was 1997.

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:18.030
That didn’t matter at first, though.

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:22.850
Hong Kong grew and grew and grew and grew
into one of the richest, most powerful, and

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:24.710
most developed cities in the world.

00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:27.780
It became the business center of Asia.

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:33.250
It thrived under a strong capitalistic economic
model but then, as forever drew nearer, a

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:35.410
question loomed over the city.

00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:37.540
Was it really going to go back to China?

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:41.760
That’s the question British Prime Minister
Margaret Thatcher had for Deng Xiaoping, China’s

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:45.120
paramount leader, when she visited Beijing
in 1982.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:49.790
The answer was effectively yes, in China’s
eyes, Hong Kong would return in 1997.

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:54.130
Now, it’s important to note that it was
only the lease of this portion, the New Territories,

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:55.800
that expired in 1997.

00:01:55.800 --> 00:02:00.280
Kowloon and Hong Kong island were ceded in
perpetuity which meant that according to the

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:04.130
terms of the agreements Britain could have
kept these areas.

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:08.360
Hong Kong as a whole, though, is small enough
already and the city had very much grown into

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:12.180
the New Territories so it was decided early
on that these negotiations were effectively

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:16.249
over what would happen to Hong Kong as a whole—it
would be too impractical to divide up the

00:02:16.249 --> 00:02:18.730
quite integrated city into different parts.

00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:23.490
Eventually, after years of back and forth,
a decision was reached in the last days of

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:28.329
1984: Hong Kong would go back to China in
1997 but the Hong Kong Way of life, with a

00:02:28.329 --> 00:02:32.769
capitalistic system and democratic government,
would remain untouched for fifty years after

00:02:32.769 --> 00:02:35.260
the handover until 2047.

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:38.930
Hong Kong would become a semi-autonomous region
of China.

00:02:38.930 --> 00:02:41.099
With that, a clock started ticking.

00:02:41.099 --> 00:02:45.499
There were 12 years, 6 months, and 12 days
left until Hong Kong would change from British

00:02:45.499 --> 00:02:46.700
to Chinese.

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:50.859
In those twelve and a half years an enormous
amount of planning and preparation was conducted

00:02:50.859 --> 00:02:54.959
in order to carry out one of the only modern
instances of the change in sovereignty of

00:02:54.959 --> 00:02:59.700
a city as large and influential as Hong Kong
between two countries so different.

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:04.870
In fact, this was the first time a capitalistic
territory had been handed over to a communist

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:05.870
state.

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:09.540
Conducting such a monumental shift was no
easy feat.

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:13.719
As such a significant business hub, Hong Kong
based companies were some of the first to

00:03:13.719 --> 00:03:15.299
make their handover plans.

00:03:15.299 --> 00:03:20.120
There was, at the time, a lot of uncertainty
about what would happen to the territory post-Handover

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:23.370
so many companies restructured to be legally
registered elsewhere.

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:26.959
Jardine Matheson, for example, one of the
most prominent Hong Kong companies, moved

00:03:26.959 --> 00:03:31.099
their legal headquarters to Bermuda and their
stock listing to Singapore even if their de-facto

00:03:31.099 --> 00:03:33.180
headquarters remained in Hong Kong.

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:37.579
HSBC, which stands for the Hong Kong and Shanghai
Banking Corporation, legally transferred many

00:03:37.579 --> 00:03:40.290
of their assets to their London office, as
well.

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:43.859
Companies typically didn’t leave Hong Kong
but rather set themselves up to be able to

00:03:43.859 --> 00:03:45.919
in case the Handover went badly.

00:03:45.919 --> 00:03:49.959
In addition to companies, individual people
made their Handover plans as well.

00:03:49.959 --> 00:03:52.799
Now, opinions were mixed on this grand change.

00:03:52.799 --> 00:03:57.169
There was no one resounding view on whether
the change in sovereignty was good or bad

00:03:57.169 --> 00:03:58.239
for Hong Kong.

00:03:58.239 --> 00:04:03.029
When surveyed in 1991, for example, about
57% of respondents were confident in Hong

00:04:03.029 --> 00:04:05.389
Kong’s future while 35% were not.

00:04:05.389 --> 00:04:10.329
Of those 35% who were not, many chose to move
elsewhere and there began a period of mass

00:04:10.329 --> 00:04:12.319
migration away from Hong Kong.

00:04:12.319 --> 00:04:17.229
A staggering 800,000 people left in the twelve
and a half years leading up to the handover,

00:04:17.229 --> 00:04:18.449
according to estimates.

00:04:18.449 --> 00:04:22.909
Overwhelmingly, Hong Kongers settled in Australia
or Canada, likely because they were commonwealth

00:04:22.909 --> 00:04:24.780
countries, and the US.

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:30.360
Over 110,000 Hong Kongers settled in Vancouver
alone—a city still shaped by this wave of

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:31.360
mass migration.

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:35.879
Many, though, were just migrating temporarily
for a few years to gain Canadian citizenship.

00:04:35.879 --> 00:04:40.259
With this, like companies, they would have
a way to get out of Hong Kong if things went

00:04:40.259 --> 00:04:41.419
wrong.

00:04:41.419 --> 00:04:45.870
Hong Kongers could also get a special type
of passport that was first introduced in 1985

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:47.240
in the lead-up to the Handover.

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:52.060
It was called the British National Overseas
passport and it works differently to a British

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:53.060
passport.

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:57.930
It gives visa-free access to 118 countries,
fewer than a full British passport, and noticeably,

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:01.689
does not give the right for someone to live
and work in the UK.

00:05:01.689 --> 00:05:05.400
Holders can only stay in the UK for up to
six months and are not considered European

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:06.770
Union citizens.

00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:10.501
These passports are still valid today and
can be renewed for anyone born in Hong Kong

00:05:10.501 --> 00:05:11.900
prior to the handover.

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:16.039
There was a huge and increasing rush of people
at registration for these passports in the

00:05:16.039 --> 00:05:19.720
years leading up to the Handover with millions
overall being issued.

00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:23.580
While today’s Hong Kong Special administrative
region passport is more powerful in terms

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:27.930
of visa-free access to countries, holders
of British National Overseas passports, as

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:32.350
British nationals, get the same consular assistance
and protection as a full British citizen in

00:05:32.350 --> 00:05:36.310
case a holder runs into trouble outside the
UK, at least on paper.

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:40.520
For this reason, hundreds of thousands still
renew these passports to this day despite

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:41.910
their redundancy.

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:45.349
As the date of the Handover drew nearer, though,
the government started focusing on the changes

00:05:45.349 --> 00:05:46.729
they needed to make.

00:05:46.729 --> 00:05:50.820
Of course there were enormous changes to the
entire structure of government, the laws,

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:55.060
the legal system, and more but there were
also small changes to be made, plenty of which

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:56.060
were visual.

00:05:56.060 --> 00:05:58.500
For example, they needed a new flag.

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:02.039
The previous flag, which included the British
Union Jack, certainly wouldn’t work under

00:06:02.039 --> 00:06:03.040
Chinese rule.

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:07.199
A contest was held with 7,000 submissions
but all were rejected.

00:06:07.199 --> 00:06:11.030
In the end, one of the contest’s judges,
Architect Tao Ho, came up with this design

00:06:11.030 --> 00:06:14.580
which was eventually approved to be the new
flag in April 1990.

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:17.819
But there were plenty of other signs of British-ness
in Hong Kong.

00:06:17.819 --> 00:06:21.199
The emblem of the police force included a
crown and the word, “royal,” so that had

00:06:21.199 --> 00:06:22.229
to be changed too.

00:06:22.229 --> 00:06:25.910
They eventually came up with a new design
that swapped out British symbols and replaced

00:06:25.910 --> 00:06:29.210
all uniforms at a cost of $2.8 million US
dollars.

00:06:29.210 --> 00:06:32.889
The word, “royal,” in fact, was removed
from basically every institution from the

00:06:32.889 --> 00:06:36.060
Royal Hong Kong Jockey Club to the Royal Hong
Kong Golf Club.

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:39.180
They just became the Hong Kong Jockey Club
and the Hong Kong Golf Club.

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:42.219
There was just plenty of sign changing city-wide.

00:06:42.219 --> 00:06:45.039
Hong Kong’s post-boxes were another symbol
that needed altering.

00:06:45.039 --> 00:06:49.349
Most, at the time, were imported from Britain
and therefore were the iconic red pillar box

00:06:49.349 --> 00:06:51.419
style featuring a crown.

00:06:51.419 --> 00:06:55.199
Most of these were progressively removed with
all being painted green to distinguish them

00:06:55.199 --> 00:06:56.870
from the UK’s.

00:06:56.870 --> 00:07:01.150
As the days of British rule dwindled, focus
shifted towards planning the actual event

00:07:01.150 --> 00:07:02.190
of the handover.

00:07:02.190 --> 00:07:07.729
Now, this event was of enormous significance
to Hong Kong, China, Britain, and the world.

00:07:07.729 --> 00:07:12.280
The BBC described it as the biggest planned
event they had ever covered.

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:15.650
What was known was that the event would be
centered around midnight on the night of July

00:07:15.650 --> 00:07:20.219
30th, 1997—the exact moment of the reversion
to Chinese rule.

00:07:20.219 --> 00:07:24.509
As you can imagine, the two governments, Britain
and China, each crucially desiring the best

00:07:24.509 --> 00:07:29.590
possible optics for their side, negotiated
relentlessly on the details of the event.

00:07:29.590 --> 00:07:34.639
For example, there was a long dispute on which
flags would raise and lower in which order.

00:07:34.639 --> 00:07:38.230
Britain wanted the British Hong Kong flag
lowered first followed by the Union Jack flag

00:07:38.230 --> 00:07:40.949
to signify a dignified retreat from the colony.

00:07:40.949 --> 00:07:44.909
China wanted the Chinese flag to be raised
at the same time as the British flag was lowered

00:07:44.909 --> 00:07:48.139
to signify an instantaneous resumption in
sovereignty.

00:07:48.139 --> 00:07:52.229
China also wanted the new Hong Kong flag to
replace the British Hong Kong flag on the

00:07:52.229 --> 00:07:53.409
same flagpole.

00:07:53.409 --> 00:07:57.180
In the end, a compromise was reached to lower
the Union Jack and British Hong Kong flags

00:07:57.180 --> 00:08:01.249
at the same time then raise the Chinese and
new Hong Kong flags at the same time.

00:08:01.249 --> 00:08:05.240
There were also other subtle negotiated details
such as: the Convention center, where the

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:08.819
event was to be held, was designed to have
chairs facing south towards the stage.

00:08:08.819 --> 00:08:12.630
For this event, though, a stage was built
on the north side so the attendees would look

00:08:12.630 --> 00:08:15.930
north possibly for the symbolism of looking
towards China.

00:08:15.930 --> 00:08:20.759
Eventually, though, the meticulously negotiated
invites went out to thousands of dignitaries

00:08:20.759 --> 00:08:22.520
and VIP’s.

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:26.580
One week before the handover, the Royal Yacht
Britannia sailed into Victoria harbor and

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:27.580
moored to the pier.

00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:31.039
It would serve as home to Prince Charles,
who would represent the United Kingdom, during

00:08:31.039 --> 00:08:32.110
the handover.

00:08:32.110 --> 00:08:36.440
Over the next week, the majority of the handover’s
VIP’s, including Prince Charles, then British

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:40.000
Prime Minister Tony Blair, former British
Prime Minister Margaret Thatcher, American

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:44.250
Secretary of State Madeline Albright, and
plenty more arrived in the city.

00:08:44.250 --> 00:08:48.890
That week was a week of lasts: the last meeting
of the Hong Kong executive council, the last

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:52.540
changing of the guards at the British garrison,
and then it was just the last day of Britain

00:08:52.540 --> 00:08:56.400
in Hong Kong—Monday, June 30th, 1997.

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:00.830
The process kicked off with a 4pm ceremony
at Government House marking the final departure

00:09:00.830 --> 00:09:04.990
of Chris Patten, the last governor of Hong
Kong, from what had been his official residence

00:09:04.990 --> 00:09:06.620
for the previous five years.

00:09:06.620 --> 00:09:11.030
At 4:30, that first of his goodbyes was completed
and he was driven to the Royal Yacht Britannia

00:09:11.030 --> 00:09:13.160
to join the rest of the British delegation.

00:09:13.160 --> 00:09:18.850
Shortly after, around 5:30, a chartered Air
China 747 touched down at Kai Tak airport

00:09:18.850 --> 00:09:23.590
carrying Chinese President Jiang Zemin, Premier
Li Peng, and the rest of the Chinese delegation.

00:09:23.590 --> 00:09:29.010
Then, at 7:11 pm, the sun set on British Hong
Kong for the final time.

00:09:29.010 --> 00:09:33.080
Minutes later, the 4,000 lucky individuals
invited to the handover ceremony gathered

00:09:33.080 --> 00:09:35.110
at the convention center for a cocktail reception.

00:09:35.110 --> 00:09:40.190
At 8pm, an enormous fireworks display started
in Victoria harbor and then at 9 the handover

00:09:40.190 --> 00:09:43.440
guests sat down for their banquet dinner at
the Convention Center.

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:47.830
Simultaneously, around 500 Chinese People’s
Liberation Army troops were allowed over the

00:09:47.830 --> 00:09:51.410
border to move into position and ensure that
there would never be a lapse in the defense

00:09:51.410 --> 00:09:52.410
of Hong Kong.

00:09:52.410 --> 00:09:56.270
At 30 minutes to midnight, dinner was over
and the ceremony began.

00:09:56.270 --> 00:10:00.180
Prince Charles gave remarks bidding the territory
goodbye on behalf of the Queen then about

00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:04.470
a minute to midnight, God Save the Queen was
played and the British and British Hong Kong

00:10:04.470 --> 00:10:08.110
flags were slowly and simultaneously lowered,
just as negotiated.

00:10:08.110 --> 00:10:13.270
Then, in an instant, when the clock struck
midnight, Hong Kong was Chinese again, just

00:10:13.270 --> 00:10:14.930
like that.

00:10:14.930 --> 00:10:18.660
The new Hong Kong and Chinese flags were then
raised to the Chinese national anthem and

00:10:18.660 --> 00:10:21.250
Chinese President Jiang Zemin gave a speech.

00:10:21.250 --> 00:10:25.000
15 minutes past midnight, Prince Charles and
governor Chris Patten boarded the Royal Yacht

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:28.510
Britannia and sailed out of Victoria harbor
for the final time.

00:10:28.510 --> 00:10:32.190
Prime Minister Tony Blair and most of the
rest of the British delegation then boarded

00:10:32.190 --> 00:10:36.960
a chartered British Airways 777 at Kai Tak
Airport which swiftly took off bound for London

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:37.960
Heathrow.

00:10:37.960 --> 00:10:42.230
By 3:30 am, all the British forces tasked
with guarding Hong Kong until midnight had

00:10:42.230 --> 00:10:46.570
boarded flights and taken off from the Chinese
territory and with that, Britain was gone

00:10:46.570 --> 00:10:50.060
from Hong Kong, for good.

